   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-calculus-ab",
    "unit_number": 2,
    "topic_code": "2.3",
    "topic_title": "Estimating Derivatives of a Function at a Point",
    "stem": "Water is pumped into a tank. The volume V(t), in liters, is recorded at selected times t, in hours. Data as (t, V(t)): (0, 120), (2, 132), (5, 147), (9, 159), (12, 165). V is differentiable. Using the data closest to t = 4, which of the following is the best estimate of V′(4)?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "3.75 liters per hour",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This uses the distant endpoints, (165 − 120)/(12 − 0), which is the average rate over the whole 12 hours rather than a local slope near t = 4. Fix: Choose the two table times closest to the target point on either side."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "15 liters per hour",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This is the change in volume, 147 − 132, without dividing by the change in time. A derivative estimate is a rate, so the 3-hour gap must be in the denominator. Fix: Compute ΔV/Δt, dividing the output change by the input change."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "5 liters per hour",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "The table values that bracket t = 4 most closely are t = 2 and t = 5. The difference quotient (147 − 132)/(5 − 2) = 15/3 = 5 is the local slope nearest t = 4, so it estimates V′(4) as 5 liters per hour."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "3 liters per hour",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This uses t = 5 and t = 9, giving (159 − 147)/4, but that interval does not contain t = 4. It estimates the rate near t = 7 instead. Fix: Check that your chosen interval actually contains the target time before computing."
     }
    ]
   },
